<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odawa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ottawa people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Odawa, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a>, are an Anishinaabe people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>, closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potawatomi">Potawatomi</a>. Living around Georgian Bay, Manitoulin Island and northern Michigan, they were important traders and allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>. Their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontiac">Pontiac</a> led a widespread war against British rule in 1763. Odawa communities live today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, Michigan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odawa.jpg" alt="Odawa" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CJLippert · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OdaawaaAreas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ottawa</b> was chosen as capital of the Province of Canada by Queen Victoria in 1857, partly as a compromise between the rival cities of Toronto, Kingston, Montreal and Quebec.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Marksville culture developed into the Baytown culture and later the Coles Creek and Plum Bayou cultures, the Miller culture area was absorbed into the Mississippian culture by about 1000 CE, and the Saugeen people of the Bruce Peninsula may have developed into the historic <b>Odawa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hopewell-culture">Hopewell culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the killing of the <b>Ottawa</b> leader Pontiac by a Peoria in 1769, enemies attacked them.</blockquote><a href="/state/illinois-confederation">Illinois Confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>Building on earlier alliances, Shawnee, Miami, Lenape, Wyandot, <b>Odawa</b>, Ojibwe, Potawatomi and others coordinated through councils, with support from the Mohawk leader Joseph Brant, insisting on the Ohio as the boundary.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-confederacy">Western Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
